{"state": {"passage": "In real world ecosystems, there is more than one food chain for most organisms, since most organisms eat more than one kind of food or are eaten by more than one type of predator. A diagram that sets out the intricate network of intersecting and overlapping food chains for an ecosystem is called its food web. Decomposers are often left off food webs, but if included, they mark the end of a food chain. Thus food chains start with primary producers and end with decay and decomposers. Since decomposers recycle nutrients, leaving them so they can be reused by primary producers, they are sometimes regarded as occupying their own trophic level.", "question": "can a organism fill more than one trophic level"}, "questions": {"answer": {"type": "noul", "instructions": "Based on `passage`, is the answer to `question` yes?"}}, "expected": {"answer": true}, "tags": ["boolq", "id:3031", "t3arm:dev"], "language": "en"}
